---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/segmentation';
const title = "Shopify Email Segmentation Playbook — Operator Guide";
const desc = description(site, 'guides/segmentation');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is everyone in a segment eligible to receive email?",
    "answer": "No. Membership describes an audience. Channel permission, suppression, current journey state and other action checks still need to pass."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every segment update dynamically?",
    "answer": "Use dynamic rules for changing membership and snapshots for a stated cohort or assignment purpose. Recheck sending eligibility even when cohort membership is fixed."
  },
  {
    "question": "How many segments should a store have?",
    "answer": "Use the number needed for distinct maintained decisions and reporting jobs. Document owners and dependencies rather than applying a universal ceiling."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
      <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Segmentation</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Define identity, audience membership and permission to act</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">A segment is a rule for membership. It is not an identity system, proof of marketing permission or an instruction to send immediately. Build a registry that explains which data puts someone in an audience, which action changes, and which checks must still pass at dispatch.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Start with the decision a segment changes</h2>
      <p>Write the action first: replenishment education for a purchased product, access to an eligible launch, or suppression during an unresolved service issue. Record the flow or campaign consuming the segment, its owner and the expected difference in timing, content or eligibility. A reporting cohort can also be useful; label it separately from a sending audience.</p>
      <p>Avoid a universal segment-count ceiling. Complexity depends on distinct decisions and maintainability, not order volume alone. If several segments only change a paragraph, assess whether dynamic content is simpler. If the same definition serves several consumers, retain one versioned definition and document its dependencies.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Establish identity before counting people</h2>
      <p>Record the customer key and how guest checkout, imported contacts, changed email addresses and multiple stores map to it. Preserve source identifiers for investigation. Do not merge unrelated people merely because an address, company or household attribute overlaps. Document a controlled merge policy and how suppression follows a confirmed identity change.</p>
      <p>Distinguish customer, address and company-level attributes. A B2B buyer can have personal purchases as well as company purchasing authority. A subscription belongs to a defined customer or contract, not necessarily every address sharing a domain. Keep unresolved matches out of actions that require confident identity.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Separate membership from action eligibility</h2>
      <p>A person can belong to a product-interest audience while being ineligible for marketing. Evaluate channel permission, deliverability suppression, current purchase state, service exclusions and competing journey ownership for the specific action. Keep service and promotional messaging rules distinct rather than treating an order as blanket permission.</p>
      <p>Store the membership reason and exclusion reason separately. A segment count can exceed reachable recipients without being broken. Recheck volatile conditions before dispatch so a purchase, unsubscribe or cancellation after enrollment can stop an obsolete message. Define what happens to already queued steps when eligibility changes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Make the data contract explicit</h2>
      <p>For each predicate list the field path, source, data type, allowed values, timestamp, refresh method and behavior when missing. Specify whether a purchase count includes refunded, cancelled, test or wholesale orders. Define timezone and currency normalization for recency and monetary rules. An unknown value is not automatically false.</p>
      <p>Give stale and conflicting data a documented treatment. If subscription status is missing, do not silently infer cancellation. Where the action needs current inventory or permission and freshness cannot be established, defer or exclude it and surface the reason. Record who can resolve the source data rather than adding another unexplained tag.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Write lifecycle states without contradictory thresholds</h2>
      <p>Use purchaser and non-purchaser states only after specifying eligible orders. Repeat-buyer, VIP and lapsed definitions should reflect the decision they serve. Derive lapse windows from the relevant product or relationship cycle and available evidence, not a universal day count. VIP may be based on value, frequency or membership terms; state the chosen rule explicitly.</p>
      <p>Lifecycle dimensions can overlap. A repeat buyer may also be a subscriber, VIP and recent cart owner. Keep those dimensions separate instead of forcing everyone into one permanent label. Define precedence for the action in the collision policy and record which rule suppressed a competing journey.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Add product and business context carefully</h2>
      <p>A purchased SKU can support product education; a browse event is a weaker signal of interest. Keep declared preferences separate from inferred preferences and record when they were collected. For fashion, distinguish ordered size from an enduring size preference. For gifts, avoid inferring the buyer’s personal consumption from the recipient’s product.</p>
      <p>Subscription active, paused, cancelled and failed-payment states need separate actions and a trustworthy refresh source. Wholesale approval and purchasing authority require company context. Catalog overlays should answer a concrete workflow question; do not add inferred sensitive traits or promise a replenishment interval without a defensible product basis.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Use dynamic rules and snapshots for different jobs</h2>
      <p>Dynamic membership can follow current source data; a snapshot preserves who qualified at a specific time. Neither is universally superior. Use snapshots for a documented reporting cohort or experiment assignment, while rechecking action eligibility before sending. Keep an immutable assignment key separate from a changing marketing segment.</p>
      <p>Specify entry, exit and re-entry behavior. Decide whether a customer can restart after another qualifying event and how duplicate events are handled. Avoid a static import as a permanent eligibility source unless an owner maintains updates. During migration compare predicates and edge cases, not only matching audience counts.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Evaluate advanced scoring by decisions</h2>
      <p>Recency, frequency and monetary scoring can organize behavior, but its definitions depend on the observation window, order types and return treatment. A predictive score needs its meaning, coverage, update cadence and limitations documented. Do not treat an unavailable prediction as a low score.</p>
      <p>Adopt an advanced split when the team has a distinct action and a way to assess it. A large order count alone does not establish that a model will improve decisions. Compare the proposed policy with a simpler baseline and use the attribution guide when claiming a causal outcome. Avoid hardcoded supplier superiority or guaranteed lift.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Rehearse membership and dispatch separately</h2>
      <p>Proposed non-sending exercise: include fixtures for a guest identity match, a refunded first order, a recent purchaser, an unsubscribe after enrollment, overlapping VIP and cart membership, a stale subscription state and a missing product field. Record expected membership, action eligibility and the reason for each outcome.</p>
      <p>Compare the actual rule output with those expectations at entry and immediately before dispatch. Test grouped AND/OR conditions, empty values, boundary timestamps and refresh delays. Preserve the rule version and fixture results. Passing these cases validates that definition; it does not establish merchant revenue or prove every production integration is current.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Maintain a registry and an audit trail</h2>
      <p>Keep name, purpose, predicate, source fields, refresh cadence, consumers, owner, version and last verification together. Investigate sudden count changes against imports, source changes and rule edits. Audit inactive definitions against dependencies before archiving; a segment with no recent campaign may still support suppression, reporting or a seasonal journey.</p>
      <p>Archive obsolete logic with its history instead of deleting customer records to tidy the segment list. Revisit definitions when products, subscription states or permissions change. Hand new operators a membership explanation and action trace they can reproduce, plus a route to resolve unknown or stale data.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><nav aria-label="Related operator guides" class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
    <a class="link" href="/guides/flow-collision-prevention">Journey ownership and collision →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/revenue-attribution">Cohorts and measurement →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Predicate transfer →</a>
  </nav></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Segmentation FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
